    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "This essay won or received an honorable mention for The Boothe Prize for excellence in first-year writing. The Boothe Prize recognizes and rewards outstanding expository and argumentative writing by undergraduate students in first-year classes that satisfy the WR 1 requirement. In each award-winning essay, student writers demonstrate clarity of argument, excellent integration of research-based evidence, and compelling prose style.\r\nThis essay leans into the power of narrative to convey argument, tracing the stories that Martin Luther King, Jr., told about the Civil War during the early 1960s in his attempt to motivate government action against oppressive Jim Crow laws and practices. Through his own narrative, Jackson Parell highlights and analyzes King’s practice of characterizing the Emancipation Proclamation, among other Civil War moments, as a kind of unfulfilled ideological promise – a historical move toward greater racial equality made hollow by contemporary instances of racial inequality.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
